    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- SEO CONTENT -->
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <section class="roth-content">
      <h2>Roth IRA Calculator – Tax-Free Retirement Growth & 2025 Limits</h2>
      <p>Our <strong>Roth IRA Calculator</strong> helps Americans plan for a tax-free retirement by estimating the long-term growth of Roth IRA contributions. Unlike a Traditional IRA where you get a tax deduction now but pay taxes in retirement, a <strong>Roth IRA</strong> is funded with after-tax dollars — meaning all your investment growth and qualified withdrawals in retirement are <strong>completely tax-free</strong>.</p>

      <h2>What Is a Roth IRA?</h2>
      <p>A <strong>Roth Individual Retirement Account (Roth IRA)</strong> is a type of tax-advantaged retirement account authorized by U.S. law. Key features:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>After-Tax Contributions:</strong> You contribute money that you've already paid income tax on — no immediate tax deduction</li>
        <li><strong>Tax-Free Growth:</strong> Investments grow tax-free inside the Roth IRA — no capital gains tax, no dividend tax</li>
        <li><strong>Tax-Free Withdrawals:</strong> Qualified withdrawals in retirement are 100% tax-free (age 59½+, account open 5+ years)</li>
        <li><strong>No Required Minimum Distributions (RMDs):</strong> Unlike Traditional IRAs, Roth IRAs have no mandatory withdrawal age — your money can continue growing tax-free for life</li>
        <li><strong>Flexible Early Access:</strong> You can withdraw your contributions (not earnings) at any time without penalty</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>2025 Roth IRA Contribution Limits</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Under Age 50:</strong> $7,000 per year</li>
        <li><strong>Age 50 or above (Catch-up):</strong> $8,000 per year</li>
        <li><strong>Income Limit (Single):</strong> Phase-out $150,000–$165,000 MAGI; no contribution above $165,000</li>
        <li><strong>Income Limit (Married Filing Jointly):</strong> Phase-out $236,000–$246,000 MAGI; no contribution above $246,000</li>
        <li><strong>Note:</strong> Total contributions to all IRAs (Roth + Traditional) cannot exceed the annual limit</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Roth IRA vs Traditional IRA – Which Is Better?</h2>
      <p><strong>Choose Roth IRA if:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>You expect to be in a higher tax bracket in retirement than you are now</li>
        <li>You are young (more years for tax-free compounding to work)</li>
        <li>You want flexibility — no RMDs, access to contributions penalty-free</li>
        <li>Your current income is below the Roth phase-out limit</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Choose Traditional IRA if:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>You're in a high tax bracket now and expect a lower tax bracket in retirement</li>
        <li>You need the immediate tax deduction to reduce current tax liability</li>
        <li>Your income exceeds Roth IRA limits (and you don't want to do backdoor Roth)</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The Power of Roth IRA Compounding</h2>
      <p>The true power of a Roth IRA lies in <strong>decades of tax-free compounding</strong>. Consider this example: A 25-year-old contributing the maximum $7,000/year until age 65 (40 years) at an assumed 7% average annual return would accumulate approximately <strong>$1.47 million</strong> — all completely tax-free upon withdrawal. If this were in a taxable account, the same person would owe capital gains taxes on over $1 million in growth.</p>

      <h2>Backdoor Roth IRA for High Earners</h2>
      <p>If your income exceeds the Roth IRA contribution limits, you can still benefit through the <strong>Backdoor Roth IRA</strong> strategy:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Contribute to a Traditional IRA (no deduction if you or your spouse has a workplace plan)</li>
        <li>Convert the Traditional IRA to a Roth IRA ("Roth conversion")</li>
        <li>Pay taxes on any pre-tax amounts converted, then enjoy future tax-free growth</li>
      </ol>
      <p>This strategy is completely legal and used by millions of high-income professionals. Consult a financial advisor or CPA for guidance on the tax implications of your specific situation.</p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <Fragment slot="faq">
    <div class="space-y-4 text-xs sm:text-sm">
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800" open>
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none"><h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">1. What is the Roth IRA contribution limit for 2025?<span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></h3></summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">The 2025 Roth IRA contribution limit is $7,000 per year for those under 50, and $8,000 for those aged 50 or above (catch-up contribution). Income limits apply: single filers earning above $165,000 MAGI and married couples above $246,000 cannot contribute directly.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none"><h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">2. When can I withdraw from a Roth IRA tax-free?<span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></h3></summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">Qualified Roth IRA withdrawals are tax-free and penalty-free when you are at least 59½ years old AND the account has been open for at least 5 years. You can withdraw your contributions (not earnings) at any time without penalty or taxes.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none"><h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">3. What is the average return on a Roth IRA?<span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></h3></summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">The average Roth IRA return depends on what you invest in. A total market index fund (like Vanguard's VTSAX) has historically returned about 7%–10% per year before inflation. A conservative bond-heavy portfolio might return 4%–6%. Our calculator defaults to 7% as a moderate estimate.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none"><h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">4. Should I choose Roth or Traditional IRA?<span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></h3></summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">Choose Roth if you're young, expect higher taxes in retirement, or value flexibility. Choose Traditional if you're in a high tax bracket now and expect lower taxes in retirement. Our Roth vs Traditional calculator helps you compare both scenarios with your actual numbers.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none"><h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">5. What is a Backdoor Roth IRA?<span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></h3></summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">A backdoor Roth IRA is a legal strategy for high earners above the income limit: contribute to a non-deductible Traditional IRA and then convert it to a Roth IRA. You'll pay taxes on any pre-tax amounts converted, then enjoy future tax-free growth.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none"><h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">6. Can I contribute to both a Roth IRA and a 401(k)?<span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></h3></summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">Yes! You can contribute to both a Roth IRA ($7,000/year) and a 401(k) ($23,500/year for 2025) in the same year. Many financial advisors recommend maxing out the Roth IRA after getting the full employer 401(k) match, as Roth IRA provides more flexibility and tax-free growth.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none"><h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">7. Does a Roth IRA have Required Minimum Distributions (RMDs)?<span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></h3></summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">No. Unlike Traditional IRAs which require RMDs starting at age 73, Roth IRAs have no required minimum distributions during the owner's lifetime. This is a major advantage for estate planning — you can leave the Roth IRA to grow tax-free for your heirs.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>

